#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125531} :activity/id #uuid "203caeae-670f-5f01-89d7-10fbe0661dd1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048635 :topic/id #uuid "b0561e68-388e-5447-b1c3-5f3b61a61231" :topic/math-academy-id 1942} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125531 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125837 :tutorial/content "Given a function $f(x,y),$ how do we calculate the partial derivatives $f_r$ and $f_\\theta,$ where $(r,\\theta)$ are plane polar coordinates?\n\nTo do this, we can use the multivariable chain rule. First, let's write down our tree diagram.\n\n![](images/7f/7f19c4a682b30f1eea1d9c1be7da83bd77ba137173f25eb9c7e5cf253abba015.png)\n\nFrom the tree diagram, we can see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's take the partial derivatives of $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂r} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  &  &  & \\frac{∂y}{∂r} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta } & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  &  &  & \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these results into the multivariable chain rule, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1b053bdf-59d9-5c48-bb1e-43f776bb9b90" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4974 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "683354ae-e60a-5bdb-b374-4caf6c7d6268" :step/math-academy-id 30975 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125532}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125532 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124405 :question/id #uuid "4940fa22-1584-565f-81fe-dc12e5132f23" :question/math-academy-id "e-8294"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050031}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508977}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "216ca278-c544-5255-95a8-27a607e680f3" :step/math-academy-id 30976 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125562}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125533 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124595 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124406 :question/id #uuid "a0246ee6-702f-5d8d-8482-8db1c46b32ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-8295"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479105}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "3dba6958-5536-5be1-b625-df1a632c257a" :step/math-academy-id 30977 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125534}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125838 :tutorial/content "Given a function of three variables $f(x,y,z),$ we can find formulas for $f_r, f_\\theta,$ and $f_z,$ where $(r,\\theta,z)$ are cylindrical polar coordinates. The results that we need are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first and second equations are identical to the equations derived for plane polar coordinates, as we would expect. \n\nThe difference here is that we now have a third equation that deals with the $z$-dependence. But since the $z$-coordinate is the same in both Cartesian and cylindrical polar coordinates, we can simply differentiate with respect to $z$ as usual.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "733b56f3-7c9a-59a6-bc6b-ef87bf07d149" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4975 :tutorial/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule With Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "f4acd445-680f-5872-a4a3-6bda4e7738eb" :step/math-academy-id 30978 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125535}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125535 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124596 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124407 :question/id #uuid "c1e69bbe-b079-56e5-9ce0-296785d73100" :question/math-academy-id "e-8296"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479141}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "19fc48bd-f02a-5fc4-8b9f-23e3d17412b2" :step/math-academy-id 30979 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125847 :tutorial/content "Up to now, we've been using the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{r} & ={f}_{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{f}_{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {f}_{\\theta } & =-r{f}_{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r{f}_{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can treat the above as a system of equations in the \"unknowns\" $f_x$ and $f_y.$ Solving this system gives explicit expressions for $f_x$ and $f_y$ in terms of functions of $r$ and $\\theta.$\n\nFor example, suppose we want an explicit formula for $f_x.$ We can eliminate $f_y$ by multiplying the first equation by $r\\cos\\theta$ and the second equation by $\\sin\\theta$ to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r{f}_{r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =r{f}_{x}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +r{f}_{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ {f}_{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-r{f}_{x}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +r{f}_{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first gives\n\n$$\nr f_r \\cos\\theta - f_\\theta \\sin\\theta = r f_x(\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta),\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\nf_x = f_r \\cos\\theta - \\dfrac1r f_\\theta \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe can use a similar procedure to write $f_y$ in terms of functions of $r$ and $\\theta.$ Our results as summarized below:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x} &= \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial r} \\cos \\theta\n\t\t\t\t\t\t-\\dfrac{1}{r} \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin \\theta\\[1ex]\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial r} \\sin \\theta\n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ \\dfrac{1}{r} \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta}\\cos \\theta\n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e3237863-f115-5396-a03f-fd481d101761" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5358 :tutorial/title "Differentiating of a Function Given in Polar Coordinates With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "443067c1-4f4c-574c-818f-cec45ee26f17" :step/math-academy-id 33488 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125533}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125848 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have a function $f(x,y,z),$ and we wish to find formulas for $f_\\rho,$ $f_\\theta,$ and $f_\\phi$, where $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi)$ are spherical polar coordinates.\n\nUsing the multivariable chain rule, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\rho } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂ϕ} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou might want to try deriving these formulas for yourself!" :tutorial/id #uuid "d956ec78-e77b-5d2c-8e7a-97ff2e549005" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5359 :tutorial/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule With Spherical Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "64b76ae8-f6a1-53fa-b321-5f096bee3ed4" :step/math-academy-id 33489}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule With Polar Coordinates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124734}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509034} :activity/id #uuid "8192fdb4-5ea5-58a8-b9e0-07e9449bea2f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048635 :topic/id #uuid "b0561e68-388e-5447-b1c3-5f3b61a61231" :topic/math-academy-id 1942} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509022 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50fa9289-6d41-5d28-b208-877cbdbfa6c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358448} {:answer/id #uuid "667d2986-4910-56e6-9ed3-8f27b9abd76f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358649} {:answer/id #uuid "add72146-efe2-5a0e-abaa-a3a2463c3158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359259} {:answer/id #uuid "e28e7362-9db3-53ed-accd-873077451494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+r{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359755} {:answer/id #uuid "ef656522-486e-50e0-b47a-5e62e587789f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359259} :answer-field/id #uuid "aee644e2-ce96-5e60-b711-61139e62f74c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "08bad6ab-1a42-5f7a-8850-36801983c246" :question/math-academy-id "q-95047" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=xy+{y}^{2}z+x{z}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+2xz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & ={r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2rz\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9478d728-7a8c-5a18-beaa-daba72c1c2d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509028}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509028 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12433c4a-668f-57da-9582-b6ee6515dc42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508415} {:answer/id #uuid "5c05a8fd-c462-59f0-82be-578cd7e2ef16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508526} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a54554-40d8-5b1a-a681-1f39ccace6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508691} {:answer/id #uuid "d353fe5f-d6bc-5d38-8a44-d344cb894fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508730} {:answer/id #uuid "f972d2aa-60ae-5ae2-a173-f83d6fe3630e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508802}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508526} :answer-field/id #uuid "53289756-546a-50f2-80e0-c0a1e0a48184" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c9126714-6e57-5593-b5b4-4801efbfb113" :question/math-academy-id "q-95111" :question/problem "Given that $q(x,y)=xy,$ find $\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $q(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂q}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂q}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂q}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂q}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $q$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂q}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂x} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂q}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b30ffaf9-b23b-569c-9c7e-cc9390ec51b6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509032}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509032 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "255b1439-4503-5ba1-9467-b4cbf23cf837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358086} {:answer/id #uuid "2826740b-006a-5fb8-af1b-b04cb4e430c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358112} {:answer/id #uuid "8f20dc0c-2f9c-59cb-9371-1e2bab94de58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358999} {:answer/id #uuid "c17a4d57-ab7f-5cad-9c18-15918d10a4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359449} {:answer/id #uuid "ff52e99a-a36a-570b-927e-6775bb16f2f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359997}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359997} :answer-field/id #uuid "d14ca8c8-8191-546e-952e-035f83d5d2de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4a5ea0e8-c2a8-56bd-bc0a-1183f811a786" :question/math-academy-id "q-95113" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)={y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂g}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta } & =0\\,(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bad45fb5-6e01-535a-822a-7b2bcb1da7a2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509048}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7120b822-0ff6-547d-b7c2-2241a49aca6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358738} {:answer/id #uuid "7d848135-2b15-5316-9a46-e4e3b36ec08b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358845} {:answer/id #uuid "811e3aba-e8b3-5975-bf14-431f1c3fe93e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358879} {:answer/id #uuid "9029ca1e-4b6a-555a-8f91-25f0d427d82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359010} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b0a061-fad6-5c22-b008-8d6182556cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359010} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d76e27a-bad6-5130-9b46-77d20a6d4839" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4970bf30-0b07-5dcd-b3de-478f21daf6db" :question/math-academy-id "q-94999" :question/problem "Given that $h(x,y)={x}^{2}y,$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $h(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & ={r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r} & =(2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c0e7d08d-e45f-545c-a6af-cb153d69442e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509022}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fe46cca-410f-5c55-88ef-8d80e7031ebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358726} {:answer/id #uuid "ba7b642c-5011-5fe6-b8b3-8ba15e13f44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359374} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc682ef-5b2d-5c3c-8130-e2f1ce1ee474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359430} {:answer/id #uuid "e32325f6-ab86-53be-a9ca-0e5883831318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359761} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9162b2-a7fc-5e49-88a2-4b87f9188d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359975}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359430} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ebf0a96-067c-5319-889e-a652fa3fcdea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "09b6c584-862f-5f2c-8d59-a960b719bfdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95116" :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂w}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=x,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fc30f928-6c4b-5fd5-b548-a7acd91c520f"}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule With Polar Coordinates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508382}]}
